And I'm really excited about that.

00:31:54.500 --> 00:32:00.500
There's a lot of names that I see popping up that watch these services and that I haven't actually met in person.

00:32:01.500 --> 00:32:03.500
And so I'm really excited about that, too.

00:32:04.500 --> 00:32:07.500
So I think we may have a boom, you know, from when we last met.

00:32:08.500 --> 00:32:11.500
And a lot of people, we get to hug each other's necks.

00:32:12.500 --> 00:32:19.500
Nikki Parker, I can't wait to hug your neck because you always give me peace whenever I'm playing music.

00:32:20.500 --> 00:32:21.500
So I'm really excited about that, too.

00:32:22.500 --> 00:32:26.500
So tonight, I do want to give some disclaimers.

00:32:27.500 --> 00:32:28.500
Some public service announcements.

00:32:29.500 --> 00:32:33.500
It's funny, I've talked at different events, keynotes before.

00:32:34.500 --> 00:32:37.500
I always give public service announcements for whatever reason.

00:32:38.500 --> 00:32:38.500


00:32:39.500 --> 00:32:43.500
I guess it's my creature joke before we get into the sermon.

00:32:44.500 --> 00:32:46.500
But I went to bed last night at 1145 because I was working.

00:32:47.500 --> 00:32:51.500
And then I got up at 130 and worked all through the night and I haven't slept.

00:32:52.500 --> 00:32:54.500
Who knows what I'll say.

00:32:55.500 --> 00:33:01.500
And if you walk away with anything from tonight, you will know that it was most definitely not of my power.

00:33:02.500 --> 00:33:03.500
So just know that.

00:33:04.500 --> 00:33:08.500
It's somebody else working on your heart and emotions.

00:33:09.500 --> 00:33:11.500
So second public service announcement.

00:33:12.500 --> 00:33:14.500
And this is an important one, I think.

00:33:15.500 --> 00:33:16.500
I'm not a theologian.

00:33:17.500 --> 00:33:18.500
I'm not a preacher.

00:33:19.500 --> 00:33:20.500
I'm not a seminary student.

00:33:20.500 --> 00:33:22.500
In fact, I am a seminary student dropout.

00:33:23.500 --> 00:33:25.500
Or a Bible college dropout.

00:33:26.500 --> 00:33:28.500
Turns out I'm allergic to seminary.

00:33:29.500 --> 00:33:32.500
So I couldn't stay there for very long.

00:33:33.500 --> 00:33:34.500
I had to get out and do other things.

00:33:35.500 --> 00:33:49.500
But I say these things jokingly, but I do just want to be very clear that I am just a guy who is moved by the life and teachings of Jesus.

00:33:50.500 --> 00:33:53.500
I think Jesus is a very significant figure in history.

00:33:55.500 --> 00:34:08.500
I believe that he is a divine being that came to earth to show us how to live, how to be human, and how to be a part of this life and to connect with each other.

00:34:09.500 --> 00:34:22.500
So when I was asked by the Elder team to speak in July, I was excited, a bit not perplexed, but my thoughts were, what do I have to say?

00:34:23.500 --> 00:34:34.500
When it comes to spirituality and talking about Jesus, I fall under the category of you speak when you have something to say and you listen the rest of the time.

00:34:35.500 --> 00:34:41.500
Like I said, I've spoken at other events in front of crowds and I love talking to people.

00:34:42.500 --> 00:34:43.500
I love connecting with people.

00:34:44.500 --> 00:34:49.500
In fact, I timed this message today and it was like an hour and a half, so we're going to go for a while.

00:34:50.500 --> 00:34:51.500
I like to talk.

00:34:52.500 --> 00:34:53.500
Just kidding.

00:34:54.500 --> 00:34:59.500
But when it comes to talking about Jesus in a corporate context, I take pause.

00:34:59.500 --> 00:35:19.500
I take pause because when you speak into somebody's spiritual life and their spirituality, whatever it may be, you can change their life for the good or the bad.

00:35:20.500 --> 00:35:24.500
I think everybody has deconstructed a part of their faith at some point in their life.

00:35:24.500 --> 00:35:29.500
And that was due to teaching that was bad teaching.

00:35:30.500 --> 00:35:36.500
So when I was asked to speak, I gave pause and was like, what do I have to say?

00:35:37.500 --> 00:35:47.500
This could really change somebody's life, not because I've got great things to say, but because I think Jesus lived a great life.

00:35:48.500 --> 00:35:56.500
And I truly believe that things can be changed by looking at the life of Jesus.

00:35:57.500 --> 00:35:59.500
Your life can be changed by looking at how he lives his life.

00:36:00.500 --> 00:36:03.500
And I want to be an instrument that pushes that in the correct direction.

00:36:04.500 --> 00:36:05.500
So I give pause.

00:36:06.500 --> 00:36:16.500
I'll have to say, like I said, again, I'm just a guy who is moved by the words, teachings, just overall life of Jesus and who he was.

00:36:17.500 --> 00:36:21.500
So tonight we are continuing on in our study of Luke.

00:36:22.500 --> 00:36:31.500
A few weeks ago, I was given two chapters to pull from, which is like asking me to find three grains of salt in the ocean.

00:36:32.500 --> 00:36:39.500
Right. I mean, two chapters of the Gospels, two chapters of rich Jesus language.

00:36:40.500 --> 00:36:47.500
Right. Rich, life changing ways to live, how to be kind and loving to each other.

00:36:48.500 --> 00:36:49.500
It's just powerful, right?

00:36:50.500 --> 00:36:51.500
So there's so many things in these two chapters.

00:36:52.500 --> 00:36:54.500
I mean, we had the feeding of the five, big stories, right?

00:36:55.500 --> 00:36:59.500
Feeding of the 5,000, Jesus sends out the 70, the 70 return.

00:37:00.500 --> 00:37:01.500
You've got the Good Samaritan.

00:37:02.500 --> 00:37:04.500
You've got the Mary and Martha debacle, right?

00:37:05.500 --> 00:37:06.500
She was sitting at Jesus' feet.

00:37:07.500 --> 00:37:08.500
Martha was working.

00:37:09.500 --> 00:37:10.500
Like so many great stories and so many things to pull from.

00:37:11.500 --> 00:37:13.500
And I was like, man, I don't know what I'm going to pick, right?

00:37:14.500 --> 00:37:19.500
So tonight we're going to talk about the title of my talk.

00:37:20.500 --> 00:37:21.500
And I call it a talk.

00:37:21.500 --> 00:37:23.500
My wife kept telling me that it's a sermon and that you're preaching.

00:37:24.500 --> 00:37:25.500
I'm like, I don't know.

00:37:26.500 --> 00:37:27.500
I can't bite off on that big of a...

00:37:28.500 --> 00:37:30.500
I mean, that's a lot of weight with that, right?

00:37:31.500 --> 00:37:32.500
So I call it a talk.

00:37:33.500 --> 00:37:34.500
We can call it whatever we want, I guess.

00:37:35.500 --> 00:37:36.500
But we're going to discuss, for what many centuries,

00:37:37.500 --> 00:37:40.500
this story has been referred to as the Good Samaritan.

00:37:41.500 --> 00:37:45.500
And to be really honest, when they said, Josh,

00:37:46.500 --> 00:37:50.500
will you talk about something from chapters 9 and 10 in Luke,

00:37:51.500 --> 00:37:56.500
I immediately told myself, don't discuss the Good Samaritan, right?

00:37:57.500 --> 00:38:03.500
I typically like to pick things that haven't been overplayed, right?

00:38:04.500 --> 00:38:05.500
You got the radio, you listen to the radio,

00:38:06.500 --> 00:38:08.500
and there's a new song out and it's like every seventh song.

00:38:09.500 --> 00:38:10.500
The song just repeats itself.

00:38:11.500 --> 00:38:12.500
To eventually the point where you're just like,

00:38:13.500 --> 00:38:14.500
I don't want to ever hear that song again, right?

00:38:15.500 --> 00:38:16.500
And I grew up in the church.

00:38:16.500 --> 00:38:21.500
And I remember the felt boards when I was little of the Good Samaritan.

00:38:22.500 --> 00:38:24.500
You put the characters on the felt board,

00:38:25.500 --> 00:38:27.500
and then the teacher told the story of the Good Samaritan.

00:38:28.500 --> 00:38:33.500
And so just personally, I was like, don't talk about this.

00:38:34.500 --> 00:38:38.500
Well, in typical fashion, whenever I say I'm not going to do something,

00:38:39.500 --> 00:38:42.500
it typically turns the other way and I end up talking about it.

00:38:42.500 --> 00:38:45.500
So I'm about to ask you a question and expect a response.

00:38:46.500 --> 00:38:47.500
So again, it's weird, right?

00:38:48.500 --> 00:38:50.500
Being on the screen, but the Enneagram, right?

00:38:51.500 --> 00:38:52.500
Some of you may be familiar with the Enneagram.

00:38:53.500 --> 00:38:54.500
I like it.

00:38:55.500 --> 00:38:56.500
I think it's a great tool.

00:38:57.500 --> 00:38:58.500
I'm not versed in it.

00:38:59.500 --> 00:39:02.500
Very little work have I done in learning deeply about it.

00:39:03.500 --> 00:39:04.500
My wife, Jo, loves the Enneagram.

00:39:05.500 --> 00:39:07.500
She's very wise when it comes to the Enneagram.

00:39:08.500 --> 00:39:10.500
She puts in the work and learns a lot about it.

00:39:10.500 --> 00:39:14.500
So I hope I make you proud, Jo, when I talk about these things.

00:39:15.500 --> 00:39:21.500
But I am a seven with a fairly strong eight wing, right?

00:39:22.500 --> 00:39:23.500
The Enneagram's one through nine.

00:39:24.500 --> 00:39:25.500
My number is a seven.

00:39:26.500 --> 00:39:27.500
I've got a strong eight wing.

00:39:28.500 --> 00:39:32.500
And so I'm going to give you a really basic rundown of what a seven is.

00:39:33.500 --> 00:39:35.500
And all this is going to tie into how I picked the Good Samaritan,

00:39:36.500 --> 00:39:37.500
and then we're going to dive in.

00:39:37.500 --> 00:39:43.500
So a seven, I'm going to give you the key motivations and the key fears.

00:39:44.500 --> 00:39:47.500
A seven's motivation is contentedness, right?

00:39:48.500 --> 00:39:51.500
A good time, always wanting to look to the positive side of things,

00:39:52.500 --> 00:39:53.500
satisfaction, right?

00:39:54.500 --> 00:39:56.500
The fear of the seven is being trapped, like boxed in.

00:39:57.500 --> 00:39:58.500
You know, don't put me in a corner.

00:39:59.500 --> 00:40:00.500
It freaks me out.

00:40:01.500 --> 00:40:04.500
Not physically, but just I don't like walls.

00:40:05.500 --> 00:40:06.500
I don't like boundaries.

00:40:07.500 --> 00:40:11.500
You know, free and to move about in my thoughts.

00:40:12.500 --> 00:40:14.500
So that's a seven, right?

00:40:15.500 --> 00:40:16.500
And so can I break it down?

00:40:17.500 --> 00:40:19.500
Basically, if you said, called me and you said,

00:40:20.500 --> 00:40:23.500
hey, Josh, let's go skydiving tomorrow.

00:40:24.500 --> 00:40:26.500
I'd say, my answer would be,

00:40:27.500 --> 00:40:31.500
let's rent a motorcycle with a sidecar to get us to the airstrip.

00:40:32.500 --> 00:40:33.500
I'm pumped.

00:40:34.500 --> 00:40:35.500
Let's go skydiving, but let's make it even more.

00:40:35.500 --> 00:40:36.500
Let's make it even more extra, right?

00:40:37.500 --> 00:40:38.500
So that's my personality.

00:40:39.500 --> 00:40:42.500
How I landed on the Good Samaritan, however, comes from my eight wing.

00:40:43.500 --> 00:40:46.500
And the eight's core motivation, and this is, again, basic,

00:40:47.500 --> 00:40:48.500
is being like a protector.

00:40:49.500 --> 00:40:51.500
Someone who stands up for the oppressed, protects those in their tribe.

00:40:52.500 --> 00:40:54.500
Someone who's not afraid of confrontation

00:40:55.500 --> 00:40:57.500
and actually kind of enjoys confrontation a little bit.

00:40:58.500 --> 00:41:02.500
And I definitely have those pieces in my life.

00:41:02.500 --> 00:41:04.500
So that is how I landed on this passage,

00:41:05.500 --> 00:41:08.500
despite my initial thought of, don't do this.

00:41:09.500 --> 00:41:11.500
And I also thought, you know, there's a lot of people

00:41:12.500 --> 00:41:13.500
that may not have grown up in church

00:41:14.500 --> 00:41:15.500
and may not be familiar with this story,

00:41:16.500 --> 00:41:17.500
and it is a powerful story.

00:41:18.500 --> 00:41:20.500
Like, oh my goodness, it was moving.

00:41:21.500 --> 00:41:23.500
I read the passage so many times in the past few weeks.

00:41:24.500 --> 00:41:28.500
I even downloaded an audio book of the Book of Luke, chapter 10.

00:41:29.500 --> 00:41:31.500
The guy that read it had an English accent.

00:41:32.500 --> 00:41:34.500
It really made the text pop.

00:41:35.500 --> 00:41:39.500
But I don't think that it necessarily assisted in my understanding

00:41:40.500 --> 00:41:41.500
or centering around the passage.

00:41:42.500 --> 00:41:43.500
But it was nice.

00:41:44.500 --> 00:41:47.500
It was relaxing to listen to a different accent read the Bible

00:41:48.500 --> 00:41:50.500
than what I'm used to.

00:41:51.500 --> 00:41:53.500
But there is one line in the story, and we're going to get to it.

00:41:54.500 --> 00:41:55.500
It's going to take us a minute to get there,

00:41:56.500 --> 00:41:57.500
but we're going to get to it.

00:41:58.500 --> 00:42:00.500
And when you really look at it, it is like a megaphone

00:42:00.500 --> 00:42:02.500
that is life-changing.

00:42:03.500 --> 00:42:05.500
And I've read this story my whole life,

00:42:06.500 --> 00:42:08.500
and it's never jumped out at me like that.

00:42:09.500 --> 00:42:10.500
But it's huge, right?

00:42:11.500 --> 00:42:13.500
So, okay, so let's read the passage.

00:42:14.500 --> 00:42:15.500
We're going to dive in.

00:42:16.500 --> 00:42:17.500
I'm going to pull it up here.

00:42:18.500 --> 00:42:19.500
We're going to talk about what Jesus did to me this week,

00:42:20.500 --> 00:42:22.500
and hopefully I can portray that in a way

00:42:23.500 --> 00:42:25.500
to where He's going to change something in your life.

00:42:26.500 --> 00:42:27.500
Oh, wait, I've got, I'm looking at my notes here.

00:42:28.500 --> 00:42:29.500
I've got one more disclaimer.

00:42:30.500 --> 00:42:33.500
This is maybe my one really strong theological stance

00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:35.500
that I'll share tonight.

00:42:36.500 --> 00:42:37.500
And this is it.

00:42:38.500 --> 00:42:39.500
So, if you read the Bible,

00:42:40.500 --> 00:42:42.500
anywhere in the Bible, anything in the Bible,

00:42:43.500 --> 00:42:46.500
if it doesn't look like Jesus,

00:42:47.500 --> 00:42:48.500
then you read it wrong.

00:42:49.500 --> 00:42:50.500
Okay, I'm going to say it again.

00:42:51.500 --> 00:42:52.500
If what you read in the Bible,

00:42:53.500 --> 00:42:54.500
anywhere in the Bible,

00:42:55.500 --> 00:42:56.500
doesn't look like Jesus,

00:42:57.500 --> 00:42:58.500
then you read it wrong.

00:42:58.500 --> 00:43:00.500
Okay, and the passage we're going to read tonight

00:43:01.500 --> 00:43:02.500
is going to be easy to keep that in mind

00:43:03.500 --> 00:43:05.500
because it is literally Jesus telling the story.

00:43:06.500 --> 00:43:08.500
But try that, you know, when you read passages at home.

00:43:09.500 --> 00:43:10.500
Try to read it and say,

00:43:11.500 --> 00:43:12.500
did I read this in a way

00:43:13.500 --> 00:43:16.500
that portrays the Jesus of the Gospels?

00:43:17.500 --> 00:43:18.500
Right, and if you're not getting

00:43:19.500 --> 00:43:22.500
a loving, kind, compassionate,

00:43:23.500 --> 00:43:25.500
accepting, inclusive Jesus,

00:43:26.500 --> 00:43:27.500
then you read it wrong.

00:43:28.500 --> 00:43:29.500
Right, so if you read it

00:43:30.500 --> 00:43:31.500
through the lens of Jesus,

00:43:32.500 --> 00:43:33.500
it'll be life-changing,

00:43:34.500 --> 00:43:35.500
no matter what you read, okay?

00:43:36.500 --> 00:43:37.500
All right, here we go.

00:43:38.500 --> 00:43:40.500
So, chapter 10, verse 25.

00:43:41.500 --> 00:43:42.500
All right.

00:43:43.500 --> 00:43:47.500
Just then, a lawyer stood up to test Jesus.

00:43:48.500 --> 00:43:49.500
Teacher, he said,

00:43:50.500 --> 00:43:52.500
what must I do to inherit eternal life?

00:43:53.500 --> 00:43:54.500
And bam, right out of the gate,

00:43:55.500 --> 00:43:56.500
we've got conflict, right? I love it.

00:43:56.500 --> 00:43:57.500
So, this lawyer,

00:43:58.500 --> 00:43:59.500
and when they say lawyer,

00:44:00.500 --> 00:44:01.500
they're talking about a religious lawyer.

00:44:02.500 --> 00:44:03.500
So, this guy knew the Old Testament,

00:44:04.500 --> 00:44:05.500
like, backwards and forward.

00:44:06.500 --> 00:44:07.500
And so, when it says, you know,

00:44:08.500 --> 00:44:09.500
the lawyer stood up to test Jesus,

00:44:10.500 --> 00:44:11.500
he really was, like, he knew the law.

00:44:12.500 --> 00:44:13.500
The question is, what must I do

00:44:14.500 --> 00:44:15.500
to inherit eternal life?

00:44:16.500 --> 00:44:17.500
This guy knows the answer, right?

00:44:18.500 --> 00:44:19.500
You ever have those bosses

00:44:20.500 --> 00:44:21.500
or those teachers in school

00:44:22.500 --> 00:44:23.500
where they ask you a question

00:44:24.500 --> 00:44:25.500
and you can just see it on,

00:44:26.500 --> 00:44:27.500
and you're kind of in a place,

00:44:28.500 --> 00:44:29.500
like, if you don't know the answer,

00:44:30.500 --> 00:44:31.500
like, what do I say, right?

00:44:32.500 --> 00:44:33.500
I used to deliver drywall

00:44:34.500 --> 00:44:35.500
when I lived in Denver, Colorado.

00:44:36.500 --> 00:44:37.500
And we would take, I mean,

00:44:38.500 --> 00:44:39.500
thousands of pieces of drywall

00:44:40.500 --> 00:44:41.500
to new construction, hospitals,

00:44:42.500 --> 00:44:43.500
high-rises,

00:44:44.500 --> 00:44:45.500
and we had to stack them really neat

00:44:46.500 --> 00:44:47.500
because the superintendent of the job,

00:44:48.500 --> 00:44:49.500
the general contractor,

00:44:50.500 --> 00:44:51.500
wanted a neat-looking site.

00:44:52.500 --> 00:44:53.500
And sometimes he'd walk over

00:44:54.500 --> 00:44:55.500
after we were finished stacking

00:44:56.500 --> 00:44:57.500
and he'd go,

00:44:58.500 --> 00:44:59.500
does that look good to you?

00:45:00.500 --> 00:45:01.500
And I'd be like,

00:45:02.500 --> 00:45:03.500
uh,

00:45:04.500 --> 00:45:05.500
yeah, does that look good to you?

00:45:06.500 --> 00:45:07.500
Uh, yes, sir.

00:45:08.500 --> 00:45:09.500
Good to know.

00:45:10.500 --> 00:45:11.500
And then he'd walk off, right?

00:45:12.500 --> 00:45:13.500
And you're like, what?

00:45:14.500 --> 00:45:15.500
What just happened?

00:45:16.500 --> 00:45:17.500
So he knew the answer, right?

00:45:18.500 --> 00:45:19.500
The lawyer knew the answer

00:45:20.500 --> 00:45:21.500
before he asked the question.

00:45:22.500 --> 00:45:23.500
So he knows the Old Testament

00:45:24.500 --> 00:45:25.500
and he already knows the answer

00:45:25.500 --> 00:45:26.500
to the real deal, right?

00:45:27.500 --> 00:45:28.500
He's like, I'm gonna get this guy.

00:45:29.500 --> 00:45:30.500
We're gonna see if he's the real deal, okay?

00:45:31.500 --> 00:45:32.500
Verse 26.

00:45:33.500 --> 00:45:34.500
He said to him,

00:45:35.500 --> 00:45:36.500
and this is Jesus talking,

00:45:37.500 --> 00:45:38.500
what is written in the law?

00:45:39.500 --> 00:45:40.500
What do you read there?

00:45:41.500 --> 00:45:42.500
Okay, Jesus knows.

00:45:43.500 --> 00:45:44.500
This guy knows his stuff.

00:45:45.500 --> 00:45:46.500
He's a religious lawyer.

00:45:47.500 --> 00:45:48.500
He knows the Old Testament,

00:45:49.500 --> 00:45:50.500
what it says.

00:45:51.500 --> 00:45:52.500
And in a classic Jesus response,

00:45:53.500 --> 00:45:54.500
he answers with another question, right?

00:45:55.500 --> 00:45:56.500
Jesus is trying to trip him up

00:45:57.500 --> 00:45:58.500
or even the disciples

00:45:59.500 --> 00:46:00.500
who are just confused, right?

00:46:01.500 --> 00:46:02.500
They ask questions.

00:46:03.500 --> 00:46:04.500
He turns the situation around

00:46:05.500 --> 00:46:06.500
to where in the case of this lawyer,

00:46:07.500 --> 00:46:08.500
he's got to answer his own question

00:46:09.500 --> 00:46:10.500
and in turn realize

00:46:11.500 --> 00:46:12.500
the error of his worldview

00:46:13.500 --> 00:46:14.500
or just continue on

00:46:15.500 --> 00:46:16.500
in a state of confusion

00:46:17.500 --> 00:46:18.500
or not understanding, right?

00:46:19.500 --> 00:46:20.500
So verse 27.

00:46:21.500 --> 00:46:22.500
So the lawyer answered.

00:46:23.500 --> 00:46:24.500
He said,

00:46:25.500 --> 00:46:26.500
with all your heart

00:46:27.500 --> 00:46:28.500
and with all your soul

00:46:29.500 --> 00:46:30.500
and with all your strength

00:46:31.500 --> 00:46:32.500
and with all your mind

00:46:33.500 --> 00:46:34.500
and your neighbor as yourself.

00:46:35.500 --> 00:46:36.500
And he said to him,

00:46:37.500 --> 00:46:38.500
this is Jesus,

00:46:39.500 --> 00:46:40.500
you have given the right answer.

00:46:41.500 --> 00:46:42.500
Do this and you will live, right?

00:46:43.500 --> 00:46:44.500
The lawyer knows this answer as well.

00:46:45.500 --> 00:46:46.500
He's quoting Deuteronomy and Leviticus,

00:46:47.500 --> 00:46:48.500
the law.

00:46:49.500 --> 00:46:50.500
This is like his thing, right?

00:46:51.500 --> 00:46:52.500
He is the religious lawyer.

00:46:53.500 --> 00:46:54.500
He knows the text from the Old Testament

00:46:55.500 --> 00:46:56.500
You got it, man.

00:46:57.500 --> 00:46:58.500
That's it.

00:46:59.500 --> 00:47:00.500
So it doesn't stop there, right?

00:47:01.500 --> 00:47:02.500
The lawyer doesn't take that.

00:47:03.500 --> 00:47:04.500
So, you know, he's

00:47:05.500 --> 00:47:06.500
he now has to justify himself, right?

00:47:07.500 --> 00:47:08.500
So verse 29, it says,

00:47:09.500 --> 00:47:10.500
but wanting to justify himself,

00:47:11.500 --> 00:47:12.500
he asked Jesus,

00:47:13.500 --> 00:47:14.500
and who is my neighbor?

00:47:15.500 --> 00:47:16.500
Right?

00:47:17.500 --> 00:47:18.500
So he knew the answer to this question.

00:47:19.500 --> 00:47:20.500
Jesus knew he knew the answer to this question.

00:47:21.500 --> 00:47:22.500
And so at this point,

00:47:23.500 --> 00:47:24.500
Jesus was like, yeah, man, you got it.

00:47:25.500 --> 00:47:26.500
Well, he's not good with that, right?

00:47:27.500 --> 00:47:28.500
Because now he's like,

00:47:29.500 --> 00:47:30.500
I just asked this silly question

00:47:31.500 --> 00:47:32.500
and everybody knows I should know.

00:47:33.500 --> 00:47:34.500
So he goes further.

00:47:35.500 --> 00:47:36.500
Well, tell me who my neighbor is

00:47:37.500 --> 00:47:38.500
because now he wants specifics.

00:47:39.500 --> 00:47:40.500
He wants identifiers.

00:47:41.500 --> 00:47:42.500
And how often do we,

00:47:43.500 --> 00:47:44.500
in the church at large here in America,

00:47:45.500 --> 00:47:46.500
do we not like the ambiguity of Jesus?

00:47:47.500 --> 00:47:49.500
The ambiguous nature of his answers, right?

00:47:50.500 --> 00:47:51.500
We want to, well,

00:47:52.500 --> 00:47:53.500
tell us exactly what

00:47:53.500 --> 00:47:54.500
we should and shouldn't do

00:47:55.500 --> 00:47:56.500
and who we should and shouldn't love, right?

00:47:57.500 --> 00:47:58.500
So this guy's calling him out,

00:47:59.500 --> 00:48:00.500
says, well, who is my neighbor?

00:48:01.500 --> 00:48:02.500
Because I want to know that

00:48:03.500 --> 00:48:04.500
I'm still, you know,

00:48:05.500 --> 00:48:06.500
loving the right people

00:48:07.500 --> 00:48:08.500
and hating the right people, right?

00:48:09.500 --> 00:48:10.500
Or hating the wrong people.

00:48:11.500 --> 00:48:12.500
So he,

00:48:13.500 --> 00:48:14.500
he quotes the law.

00:48:15.500 --> 00:48:16.500
It was too ambiguous.

00:48:17.500 --> 00:48:18.500
He left out identifiers.

00:48:19.500 --> 00:48:20.500
So again, he tries to trip Jesus up,

00:48:21.500 --> 00:48:22.500
you know, saying, well, who's my neighbor?

00:48:23.500 --> 00:48:24.500
But in this context, right,

00:48:25.500 --> 00:48:26.500
this time period in history,

00:48:27.500 --> 00:48:28.500
the neighbor,

00:48:28.500 --> 00:48:29.500
so to this lawyer,

00:48:30.500 --> 00:48:31.500
a neighbor

00:48:32.500 --> 00:48:33.500
was those in his tribe, right?

00:48:34.500 --> 00:48:35.500
The ones that are close to him,

00:48:36.500 --> 00:48:37.500
the ones that look like him,

00:48:38.500 --> 00:48:39.500
the ones that believe like him

00:48:40.500 --> 00:48:41.500
politically and religiously, right?

00:48:42.500 --> 00:48:43.500
And so he's thinking, okay,

00:48:44.500 --> 00:48:45.500
Jesus is one of us.

00:48:46.500 --> 00:48:47.500
I'm asking him who our neighbor is.

00:48:48.500 --> 00:48:49.500
Surely Jesus is about to justify

00:48:50.500 --> 00:48:51.500
what I'm about to say

00:48:51.500 --> 00:48:52.500
and what a neighbor was

00:48:53.500 --> 00:48:54.500
and what a neighbor was not.

00:48:55.500 --> 00:48:56.500
And then again,

00:48:57.500 --> 00:48:58.500
in classic Jesus fashion,

00:48:59.500 --> 00:49:00.500
a tale.

00:49:01.500 --> 00:49:02.500
And we're going to start at

00:49:03.500 --> 00:49:04.500
verse 30 in chapter 10.

00:49:05.500 --> 00:49:06.500
Jesus replied,

00:49:07.500 --> 00:49:08.500
a man was going down from Jerusalem

00:49:09.500 --> 00:49:10.500
